        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "An alkyd resin is a synthetic polyester modified with fatty acids or vegetable oils. It is synthesized through the polycondensation of a polyhydric alcohol (polyol, like glycerol or pentaerythritol) and a polybasic carboxylic acid (like phthalic anhydride), structuralized as a dense polymeric ester network. The fatty acid chains are covalently attached to the polyester backbone, providing high flexibility, pigment wetting, and solubility in organic solvents."
      }
