          width="900" 
          height="500"
          style="width: 100%; height: auto; display: block;"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: -1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        Figure 2: Quality verification test tubes showing fluid color, viscosity dispersion, and phase stability of alkyd emulsions.
      </p>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-5">
        <h2>5. Troubleshooting Alkyd Formulation Defects</h2>
        <p>
          Formulating high-performance alkyd coatings requires precise control over raw material quality and processing parameters. Common manufacturing defects can quickly ruin a batch if not managed correctly. For a detailed guide on dispersant selection and wetting physics, consult our technical post on the <a href="/role-of-dispersants-in-paints" target="_top">role of dispersants in paint</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          One major defect is "yellowing" in interior architectural paints. Alkyds formulated with highly unsaturated drying oils containing linolenic acid (like linseed oil) undergo oxidation reactions that generate colored chromophores. This causes white paint to turn an unsightly yellow, especially in dark areas lacking solar UV exposure. Formulators resolve this by replacing linolenic-rich oils with soya fatty acids or dehydrated castor oil fatty acids, which have low yellowing tendencies.
        </p>
        <p>
          Another processing defect is premature gelation inside the reactor during synthesis. If the reaction is run too long or the ratio of tri-functional glycerol to di-functional phthalic anhydride is incorrect, the molecular weight will spike rapidly, turning the batch into an insoluble, rubbery mass. Engineers monitor this during batching by pulling regular samples to check the Acid Value and viscosity, ensuring the reaction is quenched with solvent at the precise molecular target.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section class="section" id="section-6">
        <h2>6. Comparative Analysis: Short vs. Long Oil Resins</h2>
        <p>
          The following table provides a technical comparison of the chemical composition, curing methods, and application profiles of short oil and long oil alkyd resins.
        </p>

        <div class="table-container" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 30px 0;">
          <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: #ffffff; border-radius: var(--radius-md); overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid var(--border);">
            <thead>
              <tr style="background: var(--primary); color: #ffffff; text-align: left;">
                <th style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 700; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Property</th>
                <th style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 700; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Short Oil Alkyd</th>
                <th style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 700; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Long Oil Alkyd</th>
                <th style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 700; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Chemical Significance</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 600; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Oil Length (wt%)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">30% to 45%</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">55% to 70%</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Controls resin solubility and Tg</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 600; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Primary Solvents</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Aromatics (Xylene, Toluene)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Aliphatics (Mineral Spirits, Odorless Solvents)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Governs VOC emissions and safety profile</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 600; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Drying / Curing Route</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Baking (melamine crosslinking) or fast physical drying</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Oxidative crosslinking (oxygen absorption)</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Dictates production line speeds and application tools</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);">
                <td style="padding: 15px; font-weight: 600; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Film Characteristics</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">High hardness, fast cure, low flexibility</td>
                <td style="padding: 15px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Exceptional gloss, high flexibility, slow cure</td>
